WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.383
- I'm holding you in contempt of court.

00:00:02.383 --> 00:00:05.080
- Well, there's a (bleep) surprise.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:05.913
- What'd you say?

00:00:05.913 --> 00:00:06.746
(lawyer laughs)

00:00:06.746 --> 00:00:07.780
- What?

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.030
- What'd you just say?

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.860
- Don't talk back to the
judge, quit while you're ahead.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.280
Or quit while you're slightly behind.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.535
This movie is so great.

00:00:15.535 --> 00:00:18.118
(bright music)

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.699
Hey legal eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:26.203
All right, animated, smile more.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.090
Today we are covering My Cousin Vinny

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.420
one of my favorite legal
movies of all time.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.190
And if there is one question I get

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.260
more than any other it is...

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.287
- What are you wearing?

00:00:37.287 --> 00:00:38.613
- Indochino.

00:00:38.613 --> 00:00:40.153
Let me try that again.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:44.670
If there is one comment
I get more than any other

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.380
it's whether My Cousin Vinny
is in fact legally accurate.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.610
It's certainly a favorite among lawyers.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.810
We quote this movie all the time.

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:53.920
- What is a yute?

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.220
- So, be sure to comment
in the form on an objection

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.370
which I'll sustain or overrule.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.030
And stick around until
the end of the video

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:03.560
where I give My Cousin Vinny
a grade for legal realism.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.420
So, without further ado, let's
dig in to My Cousin Vinny.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.340
- Uh-oh.
- What?

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.173
- His lights are on.

00:01:09.173 --> 00:01:13.470
(bleep) (bleep) god (bleep),
what are we gonn do now?

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:14.303
- It's probably nothing, all right?

00:01:14.303 --> 00:01:16.280
It might be a taillight
or something, just relax.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.230
- We don't have any money for bail.

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:20.380
- Bail, we don't need money
for bail, nothing's happened.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.213
Now just relax.
- Nothing?

00:01:21.213 --> 00:01:22.110
We're getting pulled over, aren't we?

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.010
You stole something, didn't you?

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.211
You're finished.
- Please, will you shut up?

00:01:24.211 --> 00:01:26.220
- Oh, I forgot some background here

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:28.270
that actually helps explain
the rest of this movie.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.300
The movie starts with
two kids from New York

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.661
who are driving through the South.

00:01:31.661 --> 00:01:34.690
They stop at a grocery
store called the Sac-O-Suds

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:37.890
to pick up some supplies,
and they accidentally leave

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.144
with a can of tuna in their
pocket without paying for it.

00:01:41.144 --> 00:01:43.320
They think that when the
police pull them over

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.210
they're being pulled over
for accidentally shoplifting

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:49.890
a can of tuna when in fact
the store manager was murdered

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.960
just after they left the store.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.640
Okay, so with that
background, that will make

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.890
the rest of this movie
make a lot more sense.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.280
- Show me your hands.
- Jesus.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.010
- Show me your hands.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:03.827
Get them up, get them up, up!

00:02:03.827 --> 00:02:06.730
Now put your hands on top of your head

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.390
and get out of the car.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.313
Out of the car!

00:02:11.361 --> 00:02:14.810
- All right, so this is a
case of mistaken identity.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.410
But it's important to
note that when a policeman

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.830
stops your car, it's
generally considered a seizure

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.530
for fourth amendment purposes.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.220
So, generally speaking the
police may not stop your car

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.110
unless they have at least
reasonable suspicion

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.990
to believe that the law has been violated.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.925
Provided that the police have
lawfully stopped the vehicle,

00:02:30.925 --> 00:02:33.850
the officer can order
the occupants to get out

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:35.930
in the name of officer safety.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:38.840
Here, all of those requirements are met.

00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:40.940
We have a murder that
was conducted by people

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.687
driving a very similar car
who fit the description

00:02:43.687 --> 00:02:46.393
of the defendants here,
so it was very likely

00:02:46.393 --> 00:02:48.560
that the police did in
fact have probable cause

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.330
to stop this car and were
correct in ordering the occupants

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.850
to get out of the car
because of the grisly nature

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:57.090
of the murder that just happened nearby.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.930
- [Male] Yeah, yeah, yeah I'm sure,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.070
it's number three and five.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:01.903
- Ridiculous.

00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:03.297
All this over a can of tuna.

00:03:03.297 --> 00:03:04.720
- [Male] Keep quiet.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.020
- Okay, so that was a
classic police lineup.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.750
There are a couple problems here.

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.510
A defendant can attack the identification

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:13.856
as denying due process

00:03:13.856 --> 00:03:16.300
when the identification,
the lineup itself,

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.120
is unnecessarily suggestive.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.160
And there is a substantial
likelihood of misidentification.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.880
If we roll the clip back,

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.330
we can see you had the two defendants here

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:28.250
look nothing like the rest
of the people in the lineup.

00:03:28.250 --> 00:03:30.360
They are several feet shorter

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:31.770
and far skinnier.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:32.750
Also additionally,

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:34.980
both of them are in the
lineup at the same time.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.350
And that is likely to be
unnecessarily suggestive

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.160
and to create a false positive

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.310
which is exactly what happened here.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.161
- Well, approving an
attorney from out of state

00:03:44.161 --> 00:03:45.620
is a pretty informal matter.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.110
I just have a few questions.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:48.690
- Huh, cool.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:50.200
This is a real thing.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.220
It's a motion called a pro hac vice motion

00:03:52.220 --> 00:03:53.740
where an out-of-state attorney

00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.050
wants to have temporary permission

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:58.670
to practice in a state
that they aren't licensed.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.920
Usually you have to be
sponsored by another attorney

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.720
but you always have to vouch

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.210
and say that you are
going to follow the laws

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.406
of that state and
jurisdiction when you apply.

00:04:08.406 --> 00:04:09.616
- How long you been practicing?

00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:12.973
- Oh about six, almost 16 years.

00:04:16.416 --> 00:04:18.093
- Any murder cases?

00:04:19.052 --> 00:04:20.754
- Quite a few, yes.

00:04:20.754 --> 00:04:22.304
- [Judge] What was the outcome?

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.223
- You know win some, lose some.

00:04:26.109 --> 00:04:29.540
- Never a good idea to lie to a judge.

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.000
The judge will find out eventually

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.940
and you can be sanctioned

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.520
both in the state that
you are trying to get into

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.500
but also the state that
you come from as well.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.473
- Mr. Gambini,

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.023
stand up.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:44.033
- Not again.

00:04:47.861 --> 00:04:49.250
- Now didn't I tell you

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.700
the next time you appear in my courtroom

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.363
that you'd dress appropriately?

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.049
- You were serious about that?

00:04:56.049 --> 00:04:58.850
(laughs)

00:04:58.850 --> 00:05:02.870
- Again, wear an actual suit
and tie when you go to court.

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:04.290
I really wish I could tell Joe Pesci

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:05.920
where I get all of my suits from.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:07.033
Indochino.

00:05:10.004 --> 00:05:12.210
- [Mona Lisa] What's all that?

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:14.890
- Trotter's files, all of 'em.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.080
- You stole his files?

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:18.350
- I didn't steal his files.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.660
Listen to this,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.040
I'm just ready to finesse him,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:22.280
I'm starting to finesse him, right,

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.113
I got him going.

00:05:23.113 --> 00:05:26.413
He offers to have his secretary
copy everything for me.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.420
- That's very impressive finessing.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:31.720
- Yeah, in reality,

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:33.100
the reason that the prosecution

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:35.390
would turn over materials to the defense

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:38.300
is they have a constitutional
obligation to do so.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.730
The government has a duty
to disclose materials,

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:42.650
certainly all exculpatory evidence

00:05:42.650 --> 00:05:44.960
but generally that translates
to all of the material

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:48.844
that the prosecution has under
supreme court precedence,

00:05:48.844 --> 00:05:52.570
specifically the 1963 case
of Brady Vs. Maryland.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:54.150
Failure to disclose that evidence,

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.090
whether willful or inadvertent,

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:57.910
is a due process violation.

00:05:57.910 --> 00:06:00.430
So the prosecutor here was
constitutionally obligated

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:01.650
to turn over his files to Joe Pesci.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:03.465
It had nothing to do with his schmoozing

00:06:03.465 --> 00:06:07.160
and to not do so would be
what's called a Brady Violation

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:09.510
and could've resulted in
overturning the conviction,

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.368
if he was able to get a conviction.

00:06:11.368 --> 00:06:13.020
- Don't you want to know why Trotter

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.780
gave you his files?

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.470
- I told you why already.

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:19.400
- He has to by law, you're entitled.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.270
It's called disclosure, you (bleep) head.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:22.960
- Hey, I just said that.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:24.490
Nice job, Marisa Tomei.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.070
- He has to show you everything

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:27.870
otherwise it could be a mistrial.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:29.920
He has to give you a list
of all his witnesses,

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:32.180
you can talk to all his witnesses.

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.230
He's not allowed any surprises.

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:35.063
- Yep.

00:06:35.063 --> 00:06:38.590
Gone are the days of trying to
ambush either party at trial.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:40.060
Prosecution has to turn over

00:06:40.060 --> 00:06:41.380
not only their evidence

00:06:41.380 --> 00:06:43.250
but as part of the pretrial filings

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:45.470
you have to disclose who the witnesses are

00:06:45.470 --> 00:06:47.080
that you're going to rely on

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:48.770
so that no one is sandbagged

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:49.800
when they actually get to trial.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.980
That doesn't help anyone.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:53.399
- Didn't teach you that
in law school either?

00:06:53.399 --> 00:06:54.310
- Guess not.

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:57.517
- Are you mocking me with that outfit?

00:06:57.517 --> 00:06:58.890
- Mocking you?

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.770
No, I'm not mocking you, judge.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:03.363
- Then explain that outfit.

00:07:06.099 --> 00:07:08.513
- I bought a suit, you seen it.

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:11.090
Now it's covered in mud.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.010
This town doesn't have a one hour cleaners

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.539
so I had to buy a new suit

00:07:14.539 --> 00:07:17.960
except that the only store
you could buy a new suit in

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:19.206
has got the flu.

00:07:19.206 --> 00:07:20.540
You get that?

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.430
The whole store got the flu.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:25.608
So I had to get this
in a second hand store.

00:07:25.608 --> 00:07:27.690
- Joe Pesci could really use a place

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:29.870
where he could get
affordable custom made suits

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:32.200
so he could actually
have more than one suit

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:33.410
when he shows up to court

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:34.865
for a capital murder case.

00:07:34.865 --> 00:07:38.300
Indochino.com promo code legal eagle.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:39.133
- I think you made your point.

00:07:39.133 --> 00:07:41.350
- In all seriousness,

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:43.180
he probably would've been better off

00:07:43.180 --> 00:07:47.240
wearing his actual normal
suit covered in mud.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.293
I've actually known lawyers

00:07:48.293 --> 00:07:51.034
who will purposefully scuff up their suits

00:07:51.034 --> 00:07:54.260
so that they seem more
approachable by the jury.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:55.979
- You on drugs?

00:07:55.979 --> 00:07:57.200
- Drugs?

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.600
No, I don't take drugs.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:01.159
- I don't like your attitude.

00:08:01.159 --> 00:08:02.830
- What else is new?

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.410
- I'm holding you in contempt of court.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:07.670
- Oh, there's a (bleep) surprise.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:08.726
- What'd you say?

00:08:08.726 --> 00:08:09.559
(laughs)

00:08:09.559 --> 00:08:10.392
- What?

00:08:10.392 --> 00:08:11.640
- What'd you just say?

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.180
- Don't talk back to the judge.

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:14.470
Quit while you're ahead

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:16.860
or quit while you're slightly behind.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:18.210
This movie is so great.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.543
- counsel, members of the jury,

00:08:20.543 --> 00:08:24.030
the evidence in this case is gonna show

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:26.880
that at 9:30 in the
morning of January 4th,

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.170
both defendants, Stanley
Rothenstein and William Gambini,

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:32.080
were seen getting out

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:37.080
of their metallic green 1964
Buick Skylark convertible

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:39.320
with a white top.

00:08:39.320 --> 00:08:40.530
The evidence is gonna show

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:41.830
that they were seen entering

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:45.053
the Sac-O-Suds convenience
store in Wazoo City.

00:08:46.090 --> 00:08:47.898
The evidence is gonna show

00:08:47.898 --> 00:08:50.650
that minutes after they
entered the Sac-O-Suds,

00:08:50.650 --> 00:08:54.440
a gunshot was heard by
three eye witnesses.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:56.610
You gonna then hear the testimony

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:58.760
of the three eye witnesses

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:01.430
who saw the defendants
running out of the Sac-O-Suds

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:04.040
a moment after the shots were heard,

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.860
getting into their faded
metallic green 1964 Buick Skylark

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:10.820
and driving off in great haste.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:13.540
- Alright, I really, really, really like

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.730
the prosecutor's opening
statement in this movie.

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:18.290
There are a couple things that he's doing

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:22.000
that actually rarely happen
correctly in the movies.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.690
Number one, this prosecutor

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.860
is giving a short summary

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.500
of what the evidence will show.

00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:29.530
Opening statements are about essentially

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:32.600
priming the jury for the story

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:34.220
that is going to be elicited

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.960
through the evidence and
through the witnesses.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.340
So this prosecutor's doing a fantastic job

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:42.710
of explaining what the evidence will show.

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:43.820
On top of that,

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:45.870
this prosecutor is doing a great job

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:48.230
of using the space in front of the jury

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:49.900
while also at the same time

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:53.043
maintaining a respectful
distance away from the jury box.

00:09:53.043 --> 00:09:54.740
If you enter the well

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:57.493
and go straight to the area
right in front of the jury,

00:09:57.493 --> 00:10:00.190
the judge is going to have
a huge problem with that.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.336
But I like that he starts a counsel table,

00:10:02.336 --> 00:10:04.690
talks about what the
evidence is going to show.

00:10:04.690 --> 00:10:06.350
And specifically to highlight

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.750
the testimony of a specific witness,

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:11.800
he then walks over
towards the witness stand

00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:15.780
and gestures to where the
testimony's going to come from.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.258
So he is being dramatic
and somewhat theatrical

00:10:19.258 --> 00:10:22.220
but within the bounds of what
someone would actually do

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.840
in a way that makes rhetorical sense.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.985
- Counselor, you wish to
make an opening statement?

00:10:27.985 --> 00:10:30.068
(laughs)

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:32.043
Counselor?

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:34.590
- I have never seen

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:37.160
lead trial counsel fall
asleep at counsel table

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:39.630
but I have 100% seen

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:41.850
other attorneys who are at
counsel table fall asleep,

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:45.050
I've seen members of the jury
fall asleep during the trial,

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:47.670
I've seen people in the
gallery fall asleep at trial.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:51.563
I've actually seen a bailiff
fall asleep in a trial once.

00:10:52.419 --> 00:10:54.380
A lot of the trial process

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.880
is actually pretty boring and mundane

00:10:56.880 --> 00:10:59.470
and it's not uncommon to
see people fall asleep,

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:02.383
just not the head trial counsel.

00:11:04.281 --> 00:11:06.431
- Everything that guy
just said is (bleep).

00:11:07.543 --> 00:11:09.060
Thank you.

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.100
- In addition to being vulgar

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:12.407
and disrespectful to the court,

00:11:12.407 --> 00:11:14.160
that's technically an argument

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.563
and not allowed as your opening statement.

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:18.905
Mr. Tipton,

00:11:18.905 --> 00:11:22.100
when you viewed the defendants

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:25.130
walking from their car
into the Sac-O-Suds,

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.140
what angle was your point of view?

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:29.320
- They was kind of walking toward me

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:30.850
when they entered the stall.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:31.880
- And when they left,

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.580
what angle was your point of view?

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:36.540
- They was kind of walking away from me.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:39.610
- So would you say you
got a better shot of them

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.490
going in and not so much coming out?

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:43.730
- You could say that.

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:44.570
- I did say that.

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:45.553
Would you say that?

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:47.760
- Yeah.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:48.920
- This is really good.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.760
The testimony was ambiguous

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.440
and so Joe Pesci here follows up

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:55.290
and confirms that the witness

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:56.886
does in fact agree with the statement

00:11:56.886 --> 00:11:58.870
that Joe Pesci has said.

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:00.480
That is a very crucial thing

00:12:00.480 --> 00:12:01.830
that a lot of lawyers miss.

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:04.783
It's so easy when you're in
the heat of cross examination

00:12:04.783 --> 00:12:07.060
that you just assume that the testimony

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:08.290
is what you think it is

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:11.650
when in reality, we're
really unreliable narrators.

00:12:11.650 --> 00:12:15.570
So it's a great idea to
make sure and summarize

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:17.920
and get the witness to actually

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:20.740
state exactly what it is
you want them to prove.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:21.573
And on top of that,

00:12:21.573 --> 00:12:22.840
when you're getting good testimony,

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:24.600
it's a great idea to get the witness

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:27.620
to repeat the thing that
is actually good for you.

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:29.720
So great job by Joe Pesci here so far.

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:33.670
- Is it possible the two yutes--

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:35.573
- Two what?

00:12:35.573 --> 00:12:37.903
(laughs)

00:12:37.903 --> 00:12:39.693
What was that word?

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.360
- What word?

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:43.740
- Two what?

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.030
- What?

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:46.690
- Did you say yutes?

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:48.600
- Yeah two yutes.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:49.433
- What is a yute?

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:52.210
- Excuse me, your honor,

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:53.043
two youths.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.810
- That's great because again

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:57.260
in the heat of cross examination

00:12:57.260 --> 00:12:59.350
sometimes people slur their words,

00:12:59.350 --> 00:13:01.430
sometimes the court
reporter can't take down

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:02.340
exactly what was said

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:04.520
or people kind of talk over each other.

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:05.870
So it is a real thing

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:08.410
where the judge or
particularly the stenographer

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.950
will actually stop the
testimony to clarify,

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:14.430
and of course this is probably
the most classic scene

00:13:14.430 --> 00:13:15.920
in the entire movie.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:17.360
And it's just hilarious.

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:19.610
Because there's a kernel of truth in it.

00:13:19.610 --> 00:13:22.850
- Is it possible the two defendants

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:24.010
entered the store,

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:27.116
picked 22 specific items
off of the shelves,

00:13:27.116 --> 00:13:30.500
had the clerk take money, make change,

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:32.220
then leave.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:36.360
Then, two different men
drive up in a similar,

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:38.060
don't shake your head, I'm not done yet,

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:38.893
wait till you hear the whole thing

00:13:38.893 --> 00:13:40.560
so you can understand this now,

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.529
two different men drive
up in similar looking car,

00:13:43.529 --> 00:13:48.529
go in, shoot the clerk,
rob him and then leave?

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:49.780
- No.

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:51.230
They didn't have enough time.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:53.349
- Well how much time
was they in the store?

00:13:53.349 --> 00:13:54.365
- Five minutes.

00:13:54.365 --> 00:13:55.300
- Five minutes.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:56.133
Are you sure?

00:13:56.133 --> 00:13:56.966
Did you look at your watch?

00:13:56.966 --> 00:13:57.799
- No.

00:13:57.799 --> 00:13:58.632
- Oh, oh, oh, I'm sorry,

00:13:58.632 --> 00:14:00.032
you testified earlier

00:14:00.032 --> 00:14:03.290
that the boys went into the store

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:05.550
and you had just begun to make breakfast,

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.097
you were just ready to eat

00:14:07.097 --> 00:14:08.500
and you hard a gunshot,
that's right, I'm sorry.

00:14:08.500 --> 00:14:09.540
So obviously it takes you

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.080
five minutes to make breakfast.

00:14:11.080 --> 00:14:11.913
- That's right.

00:14:11.913 --> 00:14:13.217
- Right, so you knew that.

00:14:13.217 --> 00:14:15.160
Do you remember what you had?

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:16.690
- Eggs and grits.

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:19.550
- So Joe Pesci is being a
little bit argumentative here

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:20.770
but what he's doing

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:24.660
is he is setting a
factual and logical trap

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:25.710
for the witness

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:27.033
to show that the witness doesn't have

00:14:27.033 --> 00:14:29.800
the kind of voracity or truthfulness

00:14:29.800 --> 00:14:30.900
that would be necessary

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:33.330
to convict for beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:34.810
If you can show that a witness

00:14:34.810 --> 00:14:37.006
is lying or can't be trusted on one thing,

00:14:37.006 --> 00:14:39.850
members of the jury are far less likely

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:42.490
to believe them on most of
the rest of their testimony.

00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:44.170
That can be really, really important

00:14:44.170 --> 00:14:46.950
for disproving the
testimony of an eye witness.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:48.020
- Instant grits?

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:51.430
- No self respecting
southerner uses instant grits.

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:53.602
I take pride in my grits.

00:14:53.602 --> 00:14:55.763
- So Mr. Tipton,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:01.210
how could it take you five
minutes to cook your grits

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:06.210
when it takes the entire
grit eating world 20 minutes?

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:07.360
- So it's a great question

00:15:07.360 --> 00:15:09.860
because there's no good
answer for the witness.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:13.000
And additionally, it relies
on facts not in evidence

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:14.940
that grits actually take 20 minutes.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.280
In real life, he would've
had to have this witness

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:19.740
establish that grits
take 20 minutes to cook.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:21.400
So you got to be really, really careful

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:24.210
when you're doing a tactic
like what Joe Pesci is doing.

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:25.780
And it can work out

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:27.560
but it's very, very dangerous on cross

00:15:27.560 --> 00:15:29.450
'cause you don't know
which way it's gonna go.

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:31.263
- So what are these pictures of?

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:34.640
- My house and stuff.

00:15:34.640 --> 00:15:36.800
- House and stuff.

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.923
And what is this brown
stuff on the windows?

00:15:41.832 --> 00:15:42.850
- Dirt.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:43.683
- Dirt.

00:15:45.770 --> 00:15:49.550
What is this rusty dusty
dirty looking thing

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:50.493
over your window?

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:54.150
- It's a screen.

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:57.608
- A screen, it's a screen.

00:15:57.608 --> 00:16:00.580
And what are these really big things

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.946
right in the middle of your view

00:16:02.946 --> 00:16:07.000
from the window of your
kitchen to the Sac-O-Suds?

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:09.063
What do we call these big things?

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:13.650
- Trees?

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:16.940
- I absolutely love what Joe
Pesci is doing right here.

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:20.130
He is using effectively
incontrovertible evidence,

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:23.228
no one's gonna dispute what
these things are in the photos

00:16:23.228 --> 00:16:26.860
but he is walking the
witness down a logical path

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:28.680
that he's not going to
be able to get out of

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:30.750
which is that there's a lot of stuff

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:32.203
between his vantage point

00:16:32.203 --> 00:16:35.550
and the events that he
is an eye witness to.

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:36.840
This is one of the reasons

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:39.610
why direct evidence through
eye witness testimony

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:42.230
can be actually very, very unreliable.

00:16:42.230 --> 00:16:43.844
But what Joe Pesci is doing

00:16:43.844 --> 00:16:47.690
is he is just having the witness agree

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:49.560
to just these tiny little facts.

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:51.840
And there's no way to dispute

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:53.767
all of the little breadcrumbs he's getting

00:16:53.767 --> 00:16:56.260
on his trail to get to
the ultimate conclusion

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:58.670
that his eye witness testimony

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:01.230
is not really that believable.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.100
So the questions are fantastic.

00:17:03.100 --> 00:17:04.376
The only thing I would've
liked to have seen

00:17:04.376 --> 00:17:07.290
is a projector or an Elmo

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:10.540
that displays these photos in blow up

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:11.920
so that the jury can also see

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:15.660
exactly what it is that the
witness is referring to.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:19.410
- You could positively
identify the defendants

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:22.060
for a moment of two seconds

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:24.931
looking through this dirty window,

00:17:24.931 --> 00:17:27.523
this crud covered screen,

00:17:28.420 --> 00:17:33.030
these trees with all these leaves on them

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:34.903
and I don't know how many bushes?

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:41.320
- It's like five.

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:44.170
- Eh eh, don't forget
this one or this one.

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:45.003
- Seven bushes.

00:17:45.003 --> 00:17:45.836
- Seven bushes.

00:17:45.836 --> 00:17:47.430
So what do you think?

00:17:47.430 --> 00:17:50.721
Is it possible you just saw
two guys in a green convertible

00:17:50.721 --> 00:17:54.563
and not necessarily these
two particular guys?

00:17:57.190 --> 00:17:58.200
- I suppose.

00:17:58.200 --> 00:17:59.660
- And what you see here

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:01.702
is that the answer that
the witness provides

00:18:01.702 --> 00:18:03.010
it doesn't matter.

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:04.900
The witness here can agree

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:06.360
or the witness can not agree

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.820
but the sign of a good cross

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:09.464
is that you don't actually care

00:18:09.464 --> 00:18:11.230
what the witness is going to say.

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:12.590
In fact, if he had said

00:18:12.590 --> 00:18:14.550
that it was a perfect identification

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:16.008
he would've lost credibility

00:18:16.008 --> 00:18:18.040
based on all of the things

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.210
that Joe Pesci just talked about here.

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:23.530
So that is the sign of a
masterful cross examination.

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:25.150
I would recommend all lawyers

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:27.313
watch this particular cross
examination of this witness.

00:18:27.313 --> 00:18:29.250
This is the best so far.

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:32.130
- I'm a special automotive
instructor of forensic studies

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.160
for the Federal Bureau of Investigation.

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:35.080
- Uh huh.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:36.570
How long you been in that position?

00:18:36.570 --> 00:18:37.540
- 18 years.

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:38.749
- Your honor,

00:18:38.749 --> 00:18:40.970
may we approach the bench please?

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:42.070
- [Judge] If you wish.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.130
- I object to this witness
being called at this time.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:55.840
We've been given no prior
notice he'd testify,

00:18:55.840 --> 00:18:57.730
no discovery of any tests he's conducted

00:18:57.730 --> 00:18:59.100
or reports he's prepared

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.480
and as the court is aware,

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:02.360
the defense is entitled to advance notice

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.110
of any witness who will testify,

00:19:04.110 --> 00:19:07.070
particularly those who will
give scientific evidence

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:09.650
so that we can properly
prepare for cross examination,

00:19:09.650 --> 00:19:11.630
as well as to give the
defense an opportunity

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:13.340
to have the witnesses's reports reviewed

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:14.690
by a defense expert

00:19:14.690 --> 00:19:16.600
who might then be in a position

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:19.240
to contradict the voracity
of his conclusions.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.220
- The prosecution here
has made a huge mistake

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:23.287
which is they haven't laid the foundation

00:19:23.287 --> 00:19:26.760
for how this guy is a credible expert

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:28.610
who can testify in court.

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:31.080
So there's really only
two kinds of witnesses.

00:19:31.080 --> 00:19:32.530
There are lay witness

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:35.810
who can only testify to
things they actually perceive

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:36.980
and then there are experts

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:38.350
who are allowed to testify

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:40.240
to their opinions on things

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:42.110
based on the evidence presented to them.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:45.122
And under Rule 702 of
the Rules of Procedure,

00:19:45.122 --> 00:19:48.470
the expert has to have
scientific, technical

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:49.560
or specialized knowledge

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:50.994
that will help the trier of fact,

00:19:50.994 --> 00:19:52.450
the testimony has to be based

00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:54.470
on sufficient facts or data,

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:55.940
the testimony has to be the product

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:57.347
of reliable principles and methods

00:19:57.347 --> 00:19:59.620
and the expert has to have

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:01.670
reliably applied the
principles and methods

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:02.880
to the case at hand.

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:06.240
So on top of FRE 702,

00:20:06.240 --> 00:20:08.340
you also have to meet the Daubert Standard

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:12.360
which is meant to give the
court the gatekeeping function

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:14.440
to prevent junk science

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:16.200
from getting into the court.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.280
- There is no way that these tire marks

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:20.400
were made by a '64 Buick Skylark.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:24.580
These marks were made by
a 1963 Pontiac Tempest.

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:25.520
- Objection, your honor,

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:26.670
can we clarify to the court

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:29.160
whether the witness is
stating opinion or fact.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:29.993
- This is your opinion?

00:20:29.993 --> 00:20:31.823
- It's a fact.

00:20:31.823 --> 00:20:34.183
- It's actually opinion.

00:20:34.183 --> 00:20:36.700
- That this kind of information

00:20:36.700 --> 00:20:38.010
could be ascertained

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.670
simply by looking at a picture.

00:20:40.670 --> 00:20:42.610
- Would you like me to explain?

00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:45.265
- I would love to hear this.

00:20:45.265 --> 00:20:46.098
- Alright.

00:20:46.961 --> 00:20:50.550
- The car that made these
two equal length tire marks

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:51.850
had positraction.

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:53.950
Can't make those marks
without positraction

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:58.380
which was not available
on the '64 Buick Skylark.

00:20:58.380 --> 00:20:59.253
- And why not?

00:20:59.253 --> 00:21:01.440
What is positraction?

00:21:01.440 --> 00:21:03.400
- It's a limited slip differential

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:05.000
which distributes power equally

00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.056
to both the right and left tires.

00:21:07.056 --> 00:21:10.370
The '64 Skylark had a regular differential

00:21:10.370 --> 00:21:13.200
which anyone whose been stuck
in the mud in Alabama knows

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:14.100
you step on the gas,

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:14.960
one tire spins,

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:16.186
the other tire does nothing.

00:21:16.186 --> 00:21:17.585
- Oh it's so good.

00:21:17.585 --> 00:21:20.650
This is a great direct
examination by Joe Pesci

00:21:20.650 --> 00:21:22.740
and great testimony by Marisa Tomei.

00:21:22.740 --> 00:21:24.250
Unlike in cross examination

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:25.880
where the attorney is the star

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:28.950
and the witness' answers
are almost irrelevant,

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:31.890
it's the absolute opposite
on direct examination.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:34.692
There, you want all eyes on the witness

00:21:34.692 --> 00:21:36.060
and all you're doing

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:39.740
is asking questions that
allow the witness to expand

00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:43.156
and you're softly guiding where
you want the witness to go.

00:21:43.156 --> 00:21:45.720
Joe Pesci's questions are fantastic.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:47.340
Essentially, what happened next

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:49.490
or can you explain why that is?

00:21:49.490 --> 00:21:50.790
What is that technical term

00:21:50.790 --> 00:21:52.020
that you just used?

00:21:52.020 --> 00:21:54.360
He's using great open ended questions.

00:21:54.360 --> 00:21:55.644
He's not leading her anywhere.

00:21:55.644 --> 00:21:58.800
And he is letting the witness be the star.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:00.488
- Now in the '60s,

00:22:00.488 --> 00:22:03.350
there were only two other
cars made in America

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:06.520
that had positraction and
independent rear suspension

00:22:06.520 --> 00:22:08.540
and enough power to make these marks.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:10.000
One was the Corvette

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:13.610
which could never be confused
with the Buick Skylark.

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:15.880
The other had the same body length,

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:19.950
height, width, weight,
wheel base and wheel track

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:21.490
as the '64 Skylark

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:25.440
and that was the 1963 Pontiac Tempest.

00:22:25.440 --> 00:22:29.980
- And because both cars were made by GM,

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:34.980
were both cars available in
metallic mint green paint?

00:22:35.540 --> 00:22:36.630
- They were.

00:22:36.630 --> 00:22:38.730
- Thank you, Miss Veto.

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:40.420
No more questions.

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:42.610
Thank you very, very much,

00:22:42.610 --> 00:22:47.343
you've been a lovely, lovely witness.

00:22:47.343 --> 00:22:48.437
- It's probably not appropriate

00:22:48.437 --> 00:22:51.210
but God it's a perfect scene.

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:54.520
How can you just not love
that direct examination

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:56.410
and Marisa Tomei's expert opinion?

00:22:56.410 --> 00:22:58.369
Fantastic, amazing.

00:22:58.369 --> 00:23:00.952
(upbeat music)

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:04.280
Okay, that was My Cousin Vinny.

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:07.993
Now it's time to give the movie
a grade for legal realism.

00:23:07.993 --> 00:23:09.630
(pounding)

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:11.950
I have a soft spot in
my heart for this movie.

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:13.060
It's so good.

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:14.770
Everyone should absolutely see it.

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:15.880
If you've never seen it before,

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:16.760
I hope I didn't ruin it.

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:18.700
First of all, you have Joe Pesci

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:21.480
giving a masterful cross examination

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:23.930
and a masterful direct examination

00:23:23.930 --> 00:23:26.050
that frankly you could take those clips

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:29.219
and use them to teach trial
advocacy in law school.

00:23:29.219 --> 00:23:33.270
You have some motion practice
and some civil procedure

00:23:33.270 --> 00:23:36.160
that is not 100% accurate
but pretty close.

00:23:36.160 --> 00:23:37.070
Of course, as a comedy,

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:38.680
they take license with a few things.

00:23:38.680 --> 00:23:40.467
Joe Pesci is floundering around,

00:23:40.467 --> 00:23:42.320
making huge mistakes

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:43.530
but it sort of fits the narrative

00:23:43.530 --> 00:23:45.919
because he's supposed to be
a really terrible attorney

00:23:45.919 --> 00:23:47.910
who just got out of law school

00:23:47.910 --> 00:23:49.360
at the beginning of this movie.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:50.352
And there's some shenanigans

00:23:50.352 --> 00:23:52.640
with experts being allowed

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:53.540
when they shouldn't be

00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:55.040
and lying to the judge

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:56.510
but it's done in such a way

00:23:56.510 --> 00:23:59.020
that it has a grain of truth to it.

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:00.190
So it's hard for me to get over

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:01.820
just how good this movie is

00:24:01.820 --> 00:24:03.581
because it's a fantastic comedy,

00:24:03.581 --> 00:24:05.406
it's a good drama

00:24:05.406 --> 00:24:08.560
and it's a nice work of legal fiction.

00:24:08.560 --> 00:24:11.250
So I don't see how I
can give My Cousin Vinny

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:13.290
anything less than an A.

00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:14.450
It's so good.

00:24:14.450 --> 00:24:15.950
Go watch this movie.

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:17.340
- The defense rests.

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:18.920
- Vincent Gambini really screwed up

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:20.760
by not wearing a proper suit to court.

00:24:20.760 --> 00:24:23.760
It's hard to overstate
the value of a great suit.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.810
All of my suits are now
custom made by Indochino.

00:24:26.810 --> 00:24:28.290
I buy them with my own money.

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:29.400
I love their stuff so much,

00:24:29.400 --> 00:24:31.440
I personally reached out to them.

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:33.318
Here's the secret to a great suit.

00:24:33.318 --> 00:24:35.550
Fit matters more than anything

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:37.770
and the only way to get
a suit that fits right

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:38.970
is to get it custom.

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.240
In the past, that has been
unbelievably expensive.

00:24:41.240 --> 00:24:42.530
But Indochino allows you

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:44.320
to make a fully custom suit

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:45.640
for a ridiculous price,

00:24:45.640 --> 00:24:47.191
often less than $400.

00:24:47.191 --> 00:24:48.390
I don't know how they do it

00:24:48.390 --> 00:24:50.030
but their suits use the best materials

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:51.772
and are perfectly tailored.

00:24:51.772 --> 00:24:54.406
I will never get an off
the rack suit again.

00:24:54.406 --> 00:24:56.800
I've had trouble with
made-to-measure suits in the past

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:58.450
because I couldn't get
the measurements right

00:24:58.450 --> 00:25:00.580
but with Indochino, I
just walked into the store

00:25:00.580 --> 00:25:03.390
and got the full attention of
a stylist for over an hour.

00:25:03.390 --> 00:25:04.350
What's up Andrew?

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:05.390
He took my measurements

00:25:05.390 --> 00:25:06.560
and a couple weeks later,

00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:08.940
I got the best fitting
suit of my entire life.

00:25:08.940 --> 00:25:10.870
It was perfect right out of the box.

